  "q": "<p>An <strong>L-tromino</strong> is three unit squares in an L shape. Prove that for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n \\ge 1\\)</span>, a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^n \\times 2^n\\)</span> board with any one square removed can be covered exactly by L-trominoes (no overlaps, nothing sticking out).</p>",
  "hint": "<p>Cut the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^{k+1} \\times 2^{k+1}\\)</span> board into four <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^k \\times 2^k\\)</span> quarters. One quarter contains the missing square; place one tromino at the centre so that it covers one square of each of the other three quarters.</p>",
  "sol": "<p><em>Base case:</em> a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2 \\times 2\\)</span> board minus one square is an L-tromino. <em>Hypothesis:</em> for some <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k \\ge 1\\)</span>, every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^k \\times 2^k\\)</span> board with one square removed can be covered. <em>Step:</em> cut a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^{k+1} \\times 2^{k+1}\\)</span> board with one square removed into four quarters of size <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^k \\times 2^k\\)</span>. The removed square lies in one quarter; put one tromino on the three centre squares of the other three quarters. Now every quarter is a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^k \\times 2^k\\)</span> board with exactly one square missing (or covered), so by the hypothesis each can be covered. <em>Conclusion:</em> by induction, the statement holds for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n \\ge 1\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>The proof is a recursive tiling. The program builds it for an <span class=\"math inline\">\\(8 \\times 8\\)</span> board and every possible missing square and checks that each square is covered exactly once (the tromino count is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\frac{4^n - 1}{3}\\)</span>):</p>\n<pre data-code=\"py\">def tile(n, hole):\n    size = 2 ** n\n    board = [[0] * size for _ in range(size)]\n    board[hole[0]][hole[1]] = -1\n    count = 0\n\n    def fill(top, left, size, hole):\n        nonlocal count\n        if size == 1:\n            return\n        half = size // 2\n        count += 1\n        tid = count\n        holes = []\n        for dr in (0, 1):\n            for dc in (0, 1):\n                r0, c0 = top + dr * half, left + dc * half\n                if r0 &lt;= hole[0] &lt; r0 + half and c0 &lt;= hole[1] &lt; c0 + half:\n                    holes.append(hole)\n                else:\n                    centre = (top + half - 1 + dr, left + half - 1 + dc)\n                    board[centre[0]][centre[1]] = tid\n                    holes.append(centre)\n        quarters = [(top + dr * half, left + dc * half) for dr in (0, 1) for dc in (0, 1)]\n        for (r0, c0), h in zip(quarters, holes):\n            fill(r0, c0, half, h)\n\n    fill(0, 0, size, hole)\n    return board, count\n\ndef ok(board, count):\n    cells = [v for row in board for v in row]\n    return cells.count(-1) == 1 and all(cells.count(t) == 3 for t in range(1, count + 1))\n\nresults = [ok(*tile(3, (r, c))) for r in range(8) for c in range(8)]\nprint(tile(3, (0, 0))[1], (4 ** 3 - 1) // 3, all(results), len(results))</pre>\n<pre data-code=\"out\">21 21 True 64</pre>"
